{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Peter Goes on a Blind Date"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Are You Tracy?"}, {"type": "position", "content": 2}, {"type": "origin", "content": "scholar > Grammar - part 3 > Are You Tracy?"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 7}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe past experiences in a range of social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn6le07ob02re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present Perfect vs. The Simple Past tense"}, {"type": "describer", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "The <span class=\"strong\">Present Perfect</span> is for unfinished actions."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Present Perfect example\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"meaning\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">Peter has just talked to his date on the phone. </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"(Peter just did it and he still has his phone in his hands)\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">Frank has woken up.</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"(He is yawning and might not be standing up or he is still in bed) \\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "text", "content": "The <span class=\"strong\">Simple Past</span> is for completed and finished actions."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Simple Past example\\\"}\",\"\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">Peter talked to his date on the phone this morning.</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"(He did it a while ago, now he is doing something else)\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">Frank woke up early today.</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"(He took a shower and he is dressed now)\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Used to and Would to Talk About Past Habits"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Would</span> and <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> have rather similar meanings. They are both used to describe something that happened regularly in the past but doesn't happen any longer. </p>  However, there is an important difference between <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> and <span class=\"strong\">would</span>. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I used to drink tea all the time."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I would always stop at the tea shop after work."}, {"type": "example", "content": "He and I used to go to dinner on Fridays."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Would</span> is not used unless you know that the time frame is in the past, while <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> does not require this. Read the following example:</br> <span class=\"italic\"> We would visit India every summer when we were children.</span></p>  However, when <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> replaces <span class=\"strong\">would</span>, the same example becomes ungrammatical: </br><span class=\"italic\"> We used to visit India every summer when  we were children.</span>"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "inquisitive"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "asking questions because you want to know or learn more"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Martin was inquisitive, always asking questions."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le07ob02aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about people's habits."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "realistic"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "being able to see things as they really are and to deal with them in a practical way"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Sarah had realistic plans for her future."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le07ob02aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to recognize"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to know and remember someone or something because you have seen it before"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: He knew it was her because he recognized  her face."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le07ob02aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to annoy"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make someone to feel slightly angry"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: He asked so many questions it started to annoy people."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le07ob02aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "firecracker"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "someone with a lot of energy and personality"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: She's a real firecracker!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le07ob02aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to do a double take"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to look at something twice because you are shocked at what you saw"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I would always stop and do a double take when I saw it."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le07ob02aud06"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"ty